From 83b358d96508be7abd210fc4f99dbe520814fe70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 01:54:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 82/82] resolve: a proc macro's output identifiers can see the caller's locals

instability 0.3.11 src/stable.rs:

    let doc = if let Some(ref version) = self.since {
        formatdoc! {"
            ...This API was stabilized in version {}.",
            version.trim_start_matches('v')
        }
    } ...

    MACRO<::"alloc-0_0_0"::format> error:0: Couldn't find variable name 'version'

indoc's proc macro passes `version.trim_start_matches('v')` through into a `format!`
call. The proc macro protocol doesn't carry spans, so every identifier that comes back
from the child process has an empty hygiene context. A local is only visible to
an identifier whose contexts include the local's own context, so the empty context
could never see a caller's local, and any function-like proc macro that passed one
through failed, upstream as well.

rustc keeps the input tokens' spans, and new ones default to `Span::call_site()`,
which resolves like code written at the invocation. So in the local lookup, an
identifier with no hygiene context (and no macro module) may also fall back to
a caller's local. An exact hygiene match still wins, so locals the macro's output
declares itself (also empty-context) shadow the caller's. Nothing else creates such
identifiers inside function bodies with an unmatched name, so other code resolves
as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/resolve/absolute.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/resolve/absolute.cpp b/src/resolve/absolute.cpp
index 67c72352..47b525dd 100644
--- a/src/resolve/absolute.cpp
+++ b/src/resolve/absolute.cpp
@@ -735,7 +735,12 @@ namespace
                                 rv.bind_variable( var.second );
                                 return rv;
                             }
-                            if( fallback_var == ~0u && var.first.hygiene.is_visible(src_context) ) {
+                            // An identifier without any hygiene context comes from a proc macro's output (the protocol
+                            // doesn't carry spans), and stands for call-site hygiene: it can see the caller's locals.
+                            // instability 0.3.11 `formatdoc!{"..{}.", version.trim_start_matches('v')}` passes `version`
+                            // (bound by the enclosing `if let`) through indoc's proc macro into `format!`.
+                            bool src_is_call_site = src_context == Ident::Hygiene() && !src_context.has_mod_path();
+                            if( fallback_var == ~0u && (var.first.hygiene.is_visible(src_context) || src_is_call_site) ) {
                                 // A macro body only sees locals declared before the macro's definition: a binding made
                                 // at the invocation (e.g. `StackState::Object { cnt: c } => { object_continue!() }` with
                                 // the body's `c` meaning the function's `let mut c: u8` - simd-json 0.18.1) is skipped
